From: PwndaWallet <pwndamining@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] wallet_manager: allocate change at the lowest UNUSED
 internal index, not MAX+1

`getNewInternalAddress` picked `MAX(derivation_index) + 1` for every change
address. After a lookahead pool of internal addresses has been derived but
never used -- the normal state -- that skips the entire pool and lands change
at its far end.

Observed on mainnet LTC, 2026-08-22: an internal pool of 20 (indices 0-19,
none ever used) put 4.02888049 LTC of real change at index 20. BIP-44 fixes
the address gap limit at 20, so a wallet restoring the same seed walks the
chain, sees 20 consecutive unused addresses, stops at index 19, and reports a
zero balance over funds that are demonstrably on-chain. The coins are not
lost and the seed is not wrong -- the seed backup simply stops being
sufficient to find them without a raised gap limit.

`_findReusableAddress` already encodes the correct rule ("lowest index with
ever_used = 0 and is_funded = 0") and `getNewAddress` already calls it. This
path never did. Prefer it, marking the reused row used rather than inserting
a duplicate at the same index, and fall back to MAX+1 only when nothing is
reusable -- at which point the trailing unused run is zero by construction,
so a fresh index at the end cannot strand anything.

Marker: PWNDA-PATCH-9
---
--- a/basicswap/wallet_manager.py
+++ b/basicswap/wallet_manager.py
@@ -519,6 +519,44 @@
         try:
             cursor = self._swap_client.openDB()
 
+            # PWNDA-PATCH-9: allocate the LOWEST unused internal index rather
+            # than MAX(derivation_index) + 1.
+            #
+            # Upstream takes MAX + 1. After a lookahead pool of internal
+            # addresses has been derived but never used -- the normal state --
+            # that skips every address in the pool and lands change at its far
+            # end. Observed 2026-08-22 on mainnet LTC: a pool of 20 (indices
+            # 0-19, none ever used) put real change at index 20, which is
+            # exactly one past BIP-44's standard address gap limit of 20. A
+            # stock wallet restoring the same seed walks the chain, stops after
+            # 20 consecutive unused addresses, and reports zero over funds that
+            # are demonstrably on-chain. The money is not lost, but the seed
+            # backup silently stops being sufficient to find it.
+            #
+            # _findReusableAddress already expresses the correct rule and
+            # getNewAddress already uses it; this path simply never called it.
+            # Reuse marks the existing row used instead of inserting a second
+            # row at the same index.
+            reuse_index, reuse_address = self._findReusableAddress(
+                coin_type, True, cursor
+            )
+            if reuse_address is not None:
+                cursor.execute(
+                    "UPDATE wallet_addresses SET ever_used = 1"
+                    " WHERE coin_type = ? AND derivation_index = ? AND is_internal = ?",
+                    (int(coin_type), reuse_index, True),
+                )
+                self._swap_client.commitDB()
+                self._log.debug(
+                    f"PWNDA-PATCH-9: reusing unused internal address for "
+                    f"{coin_type.name} at index {reuse_index}: "
+                    f"{reuse_address[:16]}..."
+                )
+                return reuse_address
+
+            # Nothing reusable means every derived internal address is already
+            # used, so the trailing unused run is zero and a fresh index at the
+            # end of the chain cannot strand anything.
             cursor.execute(
                 "SELECT MAX(derivation_index) FROM wallet_addresses WHERE coin_type = ? AND is_internal = 1",
                 (int(coin_type),),
